Here is the complete test file. It belongs at `my_module/tests/src/Kernel/CalculatorTest.php`.

```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Drupal\Tests\my_module\Kernel;

use Drupal\KernelTests\KernelTestBase;
use PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\Group;
use PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\RunTestsInSeparateProcesses;
use PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\TestWith;

/**
 * Tests the my_module.calculator service.
 */
#[Group('my_module')]
#[RunTestsInSeparateProcesses]
class CalculatorTest extends KernelTestBase {

  /**
   * {@inheritdoc}
   */
  protected static $modules = ['my_module'];

  /**
   * The calculator service under test.
   */
  protected object $calculator;

  /**
   * {@inheritdoc}
   */
  protected function setUp(): void {
    parent::setUp();
    $this->calculator = $this->container->get('my_module.calculator');
  }

  /**
   * Tests that the service is registered in the container.
   */
  public function testServiceIsRegistered(): void {
    $this->assertTrue($this->container->has('my_module.calculator'));
  }

  /**
   * Tests add() with a range of integer inputs.
   *
   * @param int $a
   *   The first operand.
   * @param int $b
   *   The second operand.
   * @param int $expected
   *   The expected sum.
   */
  #[TestWith([1, 2, 3])]
  #[TestWith([0, 0, 0])]
  #[TestWith([5, 0, 5])]
  #[TestWith([-3, 8, 5])]
  #[TestWith([-4, -6, -10])]
  #[TestWith([PHP_INT_MAX - 1, 1, PHP_INT_MAX])]
  public function testAdd(int $a, int $b, int $expected): void {
    $this->assertSame($expected, $this->calculator->add($a, $b));
  }

  /**
   * Tests that add() is commutative.
   */
  #[TestWith([7, 11])]
  #[TestWith([-7, 11])]
  #[TestWith([0, 42])]
  public function testAddIsCommutative(int $a, int $b): void {
    $this->assertSame(
      $this->calculator->add($a, $b),
      $this->calculator->add($b, $a)
    );
  }

}
```

A few notes on the choices:

- **Kernel is the right type here.** A service with a pure integer method has no UI and no HTTP surface, so Functional would just be slower for no gain. It is still a Kernel test rather than a Unit test because the point is that the service resolves correctly out of the container.
- **No `installEntitySchema()` / `installConfig()` calls.** Kernel tests install nothing by default, but this service touches neither entities nor config. If your real calculator reads settings, add `$this->installConfig(['my_module']);` to `setUp()` — a "config object does not exist" error is the tell.
- **`assertSame`, not `assertEquals`.** The method is typed `: int`, so identity comparison catches a string `"3"` sneaking through; `assertEquals` would not.
- **`protected object $calculator`** is a placeholder type. Replace it with the real class (e.g. `protected Calculator $calculator;` plus the matching `use`) once you have the class name — that gets you static analysis on the call sites.

If `add()` should reject or clamp overflow, that needs its own test; the `PHP_INT_MAX - 1` case above only covers the boundary that stays in range, since actual overflow in PHP silently produces a float and would fail the `int` return type.
